{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"rg -n '160|alphabet|Data.*schema|data.*length' docs/requirements/interfaces.toml project-trajectory/PROCESS.md docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml; Get-Content docs/requirements/interfaces.toml | Select-Object -First 65\"","aggregated_output":"project-trajectory/PROCESS.md:1304:`Data` is the optional short alphabet or schema pointer (≤160 characters; no\ndocs/requirements/system-requirements.toml:792:[requirement.SR-160]\ndocs/requirements/system-requirements.toml:1670:rationale = \"A DERIVED requirement, and labelled so. SN-029 asks that a run released to automation get as far as it honestly can, and that an approval it makes on a released tier leave a record naming who made it; it does not name the other calls a delegated run makes on the owner's behalf, the ones too settled to hold the run for and not settled enough to be history, so this obligation arrives through the unattended-operations lens rather than through the need's text. A call nobody is told about is the failure that lens listens for: it pages nobody, and a run that looks green is green partly because nothing looked at what it chose. Required fields and a close refusal make that record durable rather than an instruction that can be omitted. The obligation is keyed to a dial because how much of the owner's reading a run may claim is the owner's to set; it ships off, so a repository owes nothing until its owner asks. Every close owes the record, a partial close included, because every delegated run closes with one; a lane the machinery closed with no session present is refused too, and that refusal is a hold for a person to write the record rather than a strand. A malformed entry is reported rather than refused, because the record is there and readable and a refusal would hold finished work for a reporting defect. A decision is an already selected direction, so the owner either confirms it or overrules it; approval would falsely imply a new selection. A closed verdict makes that answer visible without inferring it from a free-text note, and an unknown value is shown rather than allowed to hide a call. An overrule that is not coupled to changed open work leaves the selected direction and its execution apart; coupling them in the transition change keeps both readable without a history walk or a marker convention. Who sets the verdict remains a convention, because the record cannot confer authority on its writer. A value of the dial outside its alphabet is judged before the record is read, since the reader keeps the obligation for such a value and would otherwise report a missing record in place of the real fault. The record is never a route for work: a call that is the owner's to make, or an act that cannot be undone, still reaches the owner through the process's exits, at every dial setting. Fed back to the need: SN-029's acceptance could name a record of the delegated calls a run made beside the record of its approvals; until it does, this row is derived and says so.\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml:54:#                      exit-code  a finite code alphabet a process returns\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml:68:#   data             OPTIONAL — the finite alphabet when there is one\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml:70:#                    one-clause schema pointer. Ceiling 160 characters; no\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml:820:[interface.IF-160]\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml:1806:coincident = \"\"\"The severity alphabet is the protocol a reader and a caller act on, and it is honoured by construction across the shipped checkers; the reading is the outcome.\"\"\"\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:230:detail = \"kit_python is the one interpreter probe sourced by pre-commit, commit-msg and pre-push. It runs the two project virtual-environment candidates, then the shipped developer-setup runtime search in its declared order, and takes the first executable path that reports Python 3.11 or later; every hook step runs on that path. With no qualifying interpreter it refuses the commit or push, names the developer-setup install step (overridable by KIT_DEV_SETUP_INSTALL), and runs neither an older interpreter nor a skipped floor, regardless of the privacy policy or loop marker. The candidates after the virtual-environment entries equal the shipped developer-setup runtime search. The equivalent obligation on the root launchers is stated on SR-160, not here: the hook probe is machinery of the hook floors. After the interpreter check, the probe resolves the kit scripts directory once for all three hooks: an override that names no directory refuses with no fallback; with none named, the default scripts directory, in either letter case; none found refuses. It then asks one parse of the declared policy, once per hook run, for a closed answer: the privacy gate off; on with the unwired reviewer required; on with it warned; a mixed declaration; or an unreadable one. A hook acts only on an exact answer; a mixed or unreadable declaration, a failed read or any other answer refuses the commit or push, naming the cause.\"\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:1324:detail = \"paraphrase_advisories compares a child cell's significant words (alphabetic runs of four characters or more, lowercased) against its parent's and reports when the share of the CHILD's words that also appear in the parent exceeds the declared threshold — the child is the denominator, so a long parent cannot dilute a short echoing child. Reported in its own advisory pipe, which never joins a failure set. The two watched relationships are an SR Rationale against its own Requirement and an LLR Detail against its parent SR Requirement — the pairs where the parent is unambiguous; each finding emits a stdout WARNING plus a report line with the exit code unchanged.\"\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:1619:[design.LLR-160]\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:1700:test_refs = \"TC-160\"\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:2028:sr_refs = [\"SR-160\"]\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:2032:detail = \"\"\"pick_py distinguishes found from usable: it first runs each candidate, then checks that its sys.version_info meets the 3.11 floor every kit script's tomllib import needs. This rejects a Windows python3 on PATH that is the Microsoft-Store alias stub, which exists and executes nothing. It tries both .venv layouts, bin/ for POSIX and Scripts/ for a Windows-created venv, before python3 and python on PATH, so the pinned toolchain a contributor built wins over whatever ambient interpreter PATH resolves. On refusal it names every rejected candidate and its PYWHY reason rather than reporting a bare 'not found', so the front door reports clearly instead of dying deep inside the engine at an obscure import. agent-resume.cmd's :pickpy label runs the identical two-stage probe for cmd.exe, trying .venv\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe, .venv\\\\bin\\\\python, python, then the py launcher. agent-resume.command carries no probe of its own; its whole body delegates (exec ./agent-resume.sh) and inherits the policy. Together the three root files realize, on Windows, macOS and Linux alike, the loop-resume half of SN-034's two universal contributor actions. NOT DISCHARGED: SN-034 names two universal actions and only loop resume has a root launcher today. The environment-preparation half's scripts (onboard.sh, dev-setup.sh) materialize under scripts/, not at the repository root SR-160's acceptance names (bootstrap.py's MAPPING; README.md's own 'Still owed' ledger states the gap), so the residual is recorded as a finding rather than fabricated as a citation.\"\"\"\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:2033:rationale = \"\"\"Realizes the acceptance clause 'does not crash cryptically' the parent states for a launcher with no working interpreter: an ambient below-floor python can boot silently past a working project .venv and die deep inside the engine at an obscure import, a failure mode invisible to any check that only reads launcher TEXT rather than running one. Two probes are the whole fix, because a launcher that only asks 'does this run' cannot distinguish a working-but-old interpreter from a working one; asking sys.version_info makes the floor an observable rather than an assumption. The residual is left unminted rather than described in the abstract, because SR-160's evidence discipline is that a cited module and symbol must exist to be read - inventing a plausible-sounding root onboarding launcher would be the exact defect the discipline exists to prevent, and the gap is real, load-bearing, and already named in this repo's own README.\"\"\"\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:2971:rationale = \"\"\"Undeclared reads off because the template ships off and a repository that never set the dial has asked for nothing. Declared but unrecognized reads record because the owner asked for something and the failure that matters is silently recording nothing; the loud refusal is config_conflicts', and the reader keeps the obligation for a caller that did not run it. One normalization serves the reader and the validator, so the two cannot disagree about which values are the dial's. The alphabet is the record module's, so the reader, the merge-slot check and the session note cannot spell it three ways.\"\"\"\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:2982:detail = \"kitlib/decisions.py imports nothing and reads no file, git or environment. record_path(run) is DECISIONS_DIR/<run>.toml under docs/decisions, the run's / becoming - and no other character rewritten; a run name carrying any other character outside the shared run-name alphabet raises ValueError naming the run and each such character. That alphabet excludes /, # and exactly the characters git refuses in any branch name one at a time: ASCII control characters, space, DEL and ~ ^ : ? * [ \\\\, matched ASCII-only, so Unicode whitespace is kept. citations(text) recognizes every character that record_path keeps in the run-name segment; because no record name carries #, its path ends at the first # after docs/decisions/, so each citation has one parse. owner_state(value) returns confirmed, overruled, the empty string for an absent owner value, or None for any other value; its strings are exact. record_findings(text) parses the text as TOML, returning one finding when it does not parse, and otherwise one finding per defect: a decision key that is not a table, an entry of that table whose id is not D-<digits>, an entry that is not a table, a key of REQUIRED_KEYS (decided, alternative, reversal_cost, why_not_escalated, review) absent or not a string, one of the first four blank after trimming, an unrecognized owner value, a retired reviewed key, an overruled entry with a blank review note, a top-level high_risk that is absent or not a list of strings, and each hoisted id the decision table lacks; an id ending -000 is skipped wherever it appears, and other entry keys are not judged. It does not raise. review_queue(text) returns (unseen, overruled, confirmed_count): unseen is each entry whose owner is absent or unrecognized and every entry carrying the retired reviewed key whatever owner value it carries, overruled is each remaining entry whose owner is overruled, and confirmed_count counts each remaining confirmed entry. Each list holds dictionaries with id, high_risk, fields and raw owner, orders hoisted entries first and then by id, excludes -000, and is empty for a text that does not parse without raising. citation(rel, id) returns <rel>#<id>; citations(text) returns the set of docs/decisions/<run>.toml#D-<digits> tokens whose id is not followed by a digit; newly_overruled(before, after) returns, in id order, entries whose written owner is overruled in the parseable after text but not in the parseable before text, reading an unparseable before side as no prior overrule and raising ValueError naming a parse error when after does not parse. A retired key or blank review note does not suppress this transition reading. owed(mode, outcomes) is true for mode record or escalate-first with at least one outcome, whatever the outcome, a partial close included. missing_record_refusal(mode, run) returns the merge slot refusal of an owed close with no record: naming the run, record_path(run) and the dial, or, for a refused run name, naming the run and the ValueError; each ends nothing was merged. session_note(mode, run) returns the empty string for off and otherwise the instruction naming record_path(run), the required keys, the two owner values with owner left unset, the hoist and the rule that the record is not an exit, with one further sentence under escalate-first preferring the exits over deciding. pending.decisions_to_review applies review_queue to the records under docs/decisions/, hoisted entries ahead of the rest, prefixes a record's findings with its file, answers no records where the directory is absent, and adds the citing work item and state folder to each overruled entry through citing_rows. gen_open_items.decisions_block renders unseen entries or the confirmed count, then overruled entries under OVERRULED_HEADING with their citing work, then findings; its Decisions to review section remains last.\"\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:2994:detail = \"_decision_record_refusal(root, branch, outcomes) is a rung of _merge_refusal, taken through _close_record_refusal directly after the per-close report rung. It reads the dial through agent_common.decision_recording from the trunk's docs and returns None at once under off. Otherwise it first returns the first config_conflicts finding, when there is one, as the refusal, so a dial value outside its alphabet is refused as configuration before the record is read. It then reads record_path(branch) off the branch's tree with git show. A branch name record_path refuses is treated as an absent record. When the record is absent and kitlib.decisions.owed holds for the branch's outcomes, it returns kitlib.decisions.missing_record_refusal: for an absent path, a refusal naming the branch, path and dial; for a refused name, a refusal naming the branch and reason. When the path is present it prints one line per record_findings finding, naming the path, and returns None. docs/decisions/ joins the surfaces an adjudication-only lane may touch without falling off the no-bar path, since an adjudicator writes its record there. agent_loop.session_body appends session_note for the lane's branch, read under the same dial, to the body of a build or an adjudication session; a review session's brief is composed by reviewer_prompt, apart from that fork, and is not handed it.\"\n# docs/requirements/interfaces.toml — INTERFACE DEFINITIONS, the kit's own.\r\n#\r\n# ONE ROW IS ONE OWNER, ITS CONSUMERS, AND A TYPED STATEMENT OF THE INFORMATION\r\n# (OI-67 ruled (a), 2026-08-29). An interface says what the information plugs\r\n# into, who it serves, and what the data is — and nothing else. A request and\r\n# its answer are two rows. A module that reads one file and writes another is\r\n# two rows. A crossing of the SYSTEM boundary is not a row here: those live in\r\n# `docs/requirements/external.toml`, and a row here ties BACK to one only when\r\n# it realizes a crossing. Everything else is an internal seam. A row whose\r\n# owner AND far side are all `external:` parties has no in-tree endpoint — no\r\n# kit module whose header can hold our reading of the surface — so it is not\r\n# a seam of this system, and `trace.py --strict` refuses it.\r\n#\r\n# THE FIELDS — what each one is, and how it is used.\r\n#\r\n#   owner            REQUIRED. The providing THING — the thing the information\r\n#                    plugs into — in the ONE spelling `consumers` uses: a module\r\n#                    path (`scripts/check`), a file or directory path\r\n#                    (`docs/stack.ini`, `docs/work/`), or an `external:` party\r\n#                    (`external:git`). NEVER a requirement or design id: the\r\n#                    requirement this seam answers to is REACHED through the\r\n#                    owner — the design rows whose `module` names it, or the\r\n#                    `Implements:` line in its header — and is not stated on the\r\n#                    row. `trace.py` fails a row whose owner is id-shaped or\r\n#                    names several things, and WARNS (never gates) on a\r\n#                    module-shaped owner that reaches no requirement either way.\r\n#                    A file- or directory-owned seam legitimately has no design\r\n#                    row; 31 rows are owned that way today.\r\n#\r\n#                    A GENERATED FILE IS OWNED BY THE MODULE THAT WRITES IT —\r\n#                    the writer is the one home a check can hold to the code;\r\n#                    a hand-edited file (a registry, `stack.ini`, `process.toml`)\r\n#                    is owned as itself and declares in its own header.\r\n#\r\n#   requestors /     THE FAR SIDE NAMES THE DIRECTION. Exactly ONE of the two\r\n#   consumers        is set, a LIST either way. `requestors` are the sides that\r\n#                    put information INTO the surface the owner defines — they\r\n#                    call the function, invoke the CLI, set the env var, write\r\n#                    the file. `consumers` are the sides that take what the\r\n#                    owner emits — they read the file, the exit code, the\r\n#                    stdout. The owner DEFINES the surface in both cases; the\r\n#                    key says which way the information runs, so one row is one\r\n#                    direction by construction. A row naming both or neither is\r\n#                    a `trace.py` finding. Name the sides you have MEASURED, and\r\n#                    an open-ended class as `external:<class>`. Every endpoint\r\n#                    cell is validated against the tree, warn-first. THE SEEDED\r\n#                    CHOICE ON THIS TREE FOLLOWS THE CHANNEL (`cli`, `env` and\r\n#                    `call` rows name requestors; the rest name consumers) and\r\n#                    is confirmed row by row as the definitions move.\r\n#\r\n#   channel          REQUIRED, CLOSED (`kitlib.spine.IF_CHANNELS`): what\r\n#                    crosses, typed —\r\n#                      cli        an invocation surface: argv flags and arguments\r\n#                      exit-code  a finite code alphabet a process returns\r\n#                      stdout     text a process emits: findings, a report\r\n#                      file       a file or directory medium with a schema\r\n#                      call       an in-process API another module imports\r\n#                      env        an environment variable or launcher slot\r\n#                      git        repository state: refs, trailers, a staged diff\r\n#                      bytes      opaque content: fetched sources, a vendored tree\r\n#                    A dial read from a config file is `file` (the medium is the\r\n#                    file; `data` names the key). A source tree walked by AST is\r\n#                    `file`. THE SEEDED VALUES ON THIS TREE ARE A CLASSIFICATION,\r\n#                    NOT A READING: every row's channel was seeded mechanically\r\n#                    from a per-row read on 2026-08-29 and is confirmed row by\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
